## Summary

`Analyser::finalise_invocation_resolutions` (`rust/tcl-compiler/src/analyser/scope.rs`) settles each recorded invocation's resolved qualified name by testing candidate names against a shared `known()` predicate:

```rust
let known = |qualified: &str| {
    procs.contains_key(qualified)
        || classes.contains_key(qualified)
        || aliases.contains_key(qualified)
        || renames.contains_key(qualified)
        || (builtins.contains(qualified.trim_start_matches(':')) && !renamed_away.contains(qualified))
};
```

The registry-builtin clause explicitly excludes a name that was renamed/deleted away (`!renamed_away.contains(qualified)`, where `renamed_away` unions `renamed_commands` values with `deleted_commands` keys). The `procs`/`classes`/`aliases`/`renames` clauses have no equivalent gate — a name is treated as "known" purely because it was *ever* declared in the file, regardless of whether a later `rename NAME {}` (or an `interp alias` deletion) made it uncallable.

## Reproducer

```tcl
namespace eval ::a {
    proc helper {} { return 1 }
}
rename ::a::helper {}
namespace eval ::a {
    proc caller {} { helper }
}
```

`rename ::a::helper {}` makes `::a::helper` an invalid command name in real Tcl — any later call to it fails with `invalid command name "::a::helper"`. The `helper` call inside `caller` should therefore be unresolved (W123), matching what already happens for a *builtin* renamed away the same way. Instead, no W123 is emitted: `known("::a::helper")` returns `true` because `procs.contains_key("::a::helper")` is `true` — `all_procs` records the proc's declaration and is never pruned or gated when the command is later deleted.

Confirmed via the project's own analyser test harness — this reproduces identically for an ordinary bareword call as shown above, and is not specific to any one invocation kind, since a class, an alias target, and a rename target all share the same ungated `known()` arms.

## Why it matters

- W123 silently under-fires for a call that would actually error at runtime, which is the diagnostic's entire purpose.
- The same `resolved_qualified_name`/`resolution_candidates` fields feed go-to-definition, find-references, rename, call-hierarchy, minify, and completion, so each of those may also point at a dead command instead of correctly reporting nothing (or falling through to whatever *does* still resolve the name, if anything does).
- The registry-builtin arm shows the intended shape of the fix already exists for one of the four cases; the other three read like an oversight rather than a deliberate scoping decision — there's no comment explaining the asymmetry.

## Suggested fix direction

Gate the `procs`/`classes`/`aliases`/`renames` clauses in `known()` the same way the builtin clause already is. `Analyser::deleted_commands` already carries deletion offsets and is already consulted for order-gating in `unresolved.rs` (`qualified_name_deleted_before`, `registry_name_deleted_before`), and the same map is already read in `scope.rs` itself (used to build `renamed_away`). Two subtleties the fix needs to preserve, matching the existing order-gating convention elsewhere in the file:

- A later re-declaration of the same qualified name after the deletion (a fresh `proc`, a fresh `rename ... NAME`) must still count as known — this is a whole-file existence question, not a per-call-site one, matching how `alias_names`/`rename_target_names` are already built in `build_w123_known_names` (`unresolved.rs`).
- A deletion recorded from inside a proc/method/class body is conditional (it only executes if that body ever runs), so it should not disqualify the declaration the way a top-level deletion does — again matching the existing convention (`Analyser::offset_is_inside_definition_body`).

## Required tests

TP/FP/TN unit coverage (alongside the existing resolution tests in `scope.rs`/`commands.rs`, or in `analyser/diagnostics/tests.rs`) plus a native `lsp_e2e` diagnostics test, at minimum:

- a bareword call through a proc renamed away earlier in the file still draws W123 (the reproducer above);
- the same for a class, an `interp alias`, and a `rename` target;
- a proc re-declared under the same qualified name *after* the deletion still resolves (no W123);
- a deletion recorded inside a proc/class body that never runs does not disqualify the declaration (matching the existing conditional-deletion convention already used for the builtin case).

## Context

Found while auditing `finalise_invocation_resolutions` during the follow-up work on #968 (the `expr` math-function namespace-resolution fix). Confirmed generic and pre-existing by reproducing the identical non-detection with a plain ordinary command with no math function involved — it is unrelated to that fix and predates it.

## Background

Issue #969 ("I230 false positive") traced to an unsound interprocedural constant-propagation seed in `rust/tcl-compiler/src/compilation_unit.rs`. During PR #970's review, two related namespace-context bugs were found and fixed: `TclOO` method bodies and `namespace eval NS { ... }` body units were both using the wrong namespace when resolving bare commands inside them, causing misattributed evidence (a call attributed to the wrong proc — one that real Tcl never actually invokes — while the real target loses that evidence too).

## The confirmed bug

`uplevel #0 { ... }` has the identical bug, confirmed live against `tclsh8.6`:

```tcl
namespace eval ::foo {
    proc helper {mode} {
        if {$mode eq "WRONG"} { ... } else { ... }
    }
    proc runIt {} {
        uplevel #0 { helper b }
    }
}
proc helper {mode} {
    if {$mode eq "a"} { ... } else { ... }
}
helper a
::foo::runIt
```

Real `tclsh8.6` output confirms `uplevel #0 { helper b }` calls the **global** `::helper`, never `::foo::helper` — but `collect_call_site_constants` currently misattributes the call to `::foo::helper` (which real Tcl never invokes this way — a phantom fold on dead evidence), while the real target `::helper` loses it as evidence too.

## Why this is harder than the `TclOO`/`namespace eval` fixes

Those two were fixed by correcting the *string* used as the resolution/caller context (forcing global for methods; threading the real target namespace into the `namespace eval` body unit's own qname — see `register_body_unit` / `lower_namespace_eval` in `rust/tcl-compiler/src/lowering/mod.rs`). `uplevel`'s body doesn't go through either of those paths: `Statement::UpFrame` (`rust/tcl-compiler/src/ir.rs`) has a `frame_shift: i32` field that's `0` for the `uplevel #0` (absolute/global) form and a positive caller-relative level otherwise — exactly the information needed to know "this call should resolve against global" — but `UpFrame`'s body is inlined into the enclosing function's own CFG blocks by the time `collect_call_site_constants` walks `block.statements`, and `frame_shift` doesn't survive that flattening to where the scan could consult it.

A correct fix needs either:
- `frame_shift == 0` preserved through CFG construction so this scan can see it, or
- a pre-CFG scan of `Statement::UpFrame` occurrences (mirroring `build_extra_call_site_scan_contexts`'s existing approach for methods and body units — building bare CFGs for out-of-band callers before the main scan runs).

This is larger than the one-line namespace-context overrides used for the other two cases, so it was deliberately left open rather than rushed.

## What's already in place

A pinned, intentionally-failing regression test documents this exact scenario and is ready to be un-ignored once fixed:

- `rust/tcl-compiler/src/compilation_unit.rs`, function `uplevel_zero_body_resolves_against_global_not_enclosing_namespace` (marked `#[ignore = "confirmed residual gap, PR #970 review: uplevel #0 body namespace context; see doc comment"]`), in the `call_site_param_constants` test module.

`uplevel N` for any relative (non-`#0`) level is a **separate, permanent** approximation and out of scope here: the target frame's namespace depends on the live call stack, which is undecidable by a single-file static analysis.

## Verification bar

Un-ignore the pinned test and confirm it passes; run the full `call_site_param_constants` module plus `cargo test -p tcl-compiler --lib` and `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings` with no new failures and no new clippy allows.

## References

- Root issue: #969
- Fix PR: #970 (see `docs/design/compiler/fp-audit-todo.md`, entry `FP-IPCP-01`, for full context including the two related fixes already landed)
- Relevant code: `rust/tcl-compiler/src/compilation_unit.rs` (`collect_call_site_constants`, `build_extra_call_site_scan_contexts`), `rust/tcl-compiler/src/ir.rs` (`Statement::UpFrame`)

## Summary

The bare `ClassName method` dispatch matcher (`find_obj_method_call_sites` in `rust/tcl-lsp-core/src/references.rs`, and its cross-file counterpart in `tcl-lsp-server/src/lib.rs`) matches call sites by exact name-set membership — a class's simple (tail) name plus its fully `::`-qualified name, inserted into a flat `cmd_set` with no namespace-scope tracking.

This means two unrelated classes sharing a simple name in different namespaces are not distinguished. For example:

```tcl
namespace eval ::a {
    oo::class create Factory {
        classmethod make {} { ... }
    }
}
namespace eval ::b {
    oo::class create Factory {
        classmethod make {} { ... }
    }
    Factory make    ;# resolves (in real Tcl) to ::b::Factory, but...
}
```

Find References / Rename on `::a::Factory::make` will incorrectly count (and, under rename, rewrite) the `Factory make` call inside `::b`, even though Tcl itself resolves that bare word to `::b::Factory`.

## Origin

Flagged by Codex automated review on PR #971 (comment on `rust/tcl-lsp-core/src/references.rs:1076`, P1). It's a pre-existing imprecision — the *same* name-set-matching approach already backs the object-command (`CLASS create NAME`) dispatch matcher, which has an identical namespace-blind spot. PR #971's classmethod-dispatch fix reuses this existing mechanism (extending it to a new dispatch shape) rather than introducing the gap fresh.

## Suggested fix direction

Namespace-scope tracking needs to be added to the bare-command dispatch scanner (`scan_obj_method_region`/`scan_obj_method_body` in `references.rs`), resolving each candidate call site's lexical namespace context before matching against a candidate class's namespace, rather than matching on name-text alone. Two possible approaches:

1. Integrate with the existing, heavier `WorkspaceIndex` namespace-path/import resolution machinery already used for proc/command references (see `workspace_index.rs`'s `invocations_of` and the `bare_call_without_path_does_not_reach_unrelated_namespace` test).
2. Build a lighter local namespace-tracking pass within the lexical bare-command scanner itself (track `namespace eval` nesting while walking the token stream).

Either approach should also fix the *pre-existing* object-command (`CLASS create NAME`) dispatch matcher's identical gap, since they share the underlying mechanism — worth fixing both at once rather than just the classmethod case.

## Current mitigation

Documented as a known failure mode in `docs/kcs/features/kcs-feature-references.md`. Not otherwise mitigated.

## Summary

Clicking a TclOO method or classmethod code lens resolves to a `tcl-lsp.showReferences` command via `codeLens/resolve`, but the locations it opens come from a narrower resolution path than manually invoking "Find All References" on the same symbol — so the lens click can show fewer results, in any workspace where the method/classmethod's references span multiple files.

## Root cause

`code_lens_resolve` (`rust/tcl-lsp-server/src/lib.rs`) resolves the clicked lens's locations via `reference_locations_at`, which calls only:

1. `core_references::references(...)` — the single-document resolver, and
2. `self.cross_document_references(...)` — the generic cross-file resolver for procs/classes/variables.

It never calls `cross_file_method_and_consumer_references` (or the `cross_file_method_references` / `cross_file_consumer_method_references` it wraps) — the method/classmethod-specific cross-file resolvers that the `references()` LSP handler *does* call for a manual "Find All References" request on the same cursor position. So a method or classmethod whose callers live in another file — an override in a sibling document, an inheriting subclass's own dispatch, or a pure-consumer document that only calls it — is invisible to a lens click, even though "Find All References" on the exact same declaration correctly finds it.

## Repro

```tcl
// animal.tcl
oo::class create Animal {
    method speak {} { return "..." }
}
```
```tcl
// dog.tcl (sibling document)
oo::class create Dog {
    superclass Animal
    method speak {} { return "woof" }
}
set d [Dog new]
$d speak
```

With both documents open in the workspace: the code lens above `Animal`'s `speak` declaration shows some count and, on click, opens a reference panel — but that panel is missing `dog.tcl`'s override declaration and `$d speak` call site, both of which "Find All References" (right-click, same declaration) correctly includes.

## Suggested fix direction

Either have `reference_locations_at` also call the method-specific cross-file resolvers (mirroring what `references()`'s handler does via `cross_file_method_and_consumer_references`), or refactor so both entry points share one code path that always includes the method/classmethod cross-file layer. The lens's own *displayed count* (computed separately in `code_lens.rs`'s `emit_class_member_lenses` → `member_ref_count` → `method_references_for_class`, same-document only) has the identical limitation and should be fixed the same way, so the number shown and the locations opened on click stay consistent with each other **and** with "Find All References" on the same symbol.

## The false positive

Found running down the two corpus survivors of the #1129 fix (PR #1175): `ruff`'s `Formatter::ResolvableReference?` draws an always-false `info exists` finding on `ref`, but `ref` is defined by the callee:

```tcl
method ResolvableReference? {lookup} {
    if {[my Reference? $lookup ref]} { ... $ref ... }
}
method Reference? {lookup refvar} {
    upvar 1 $refvar ref
    ... set ref ... ;# defines `ref` in the caller's method frame on a hit
}
```

Oracle (9.0.4 + 8.6.14): after `my Reference? $lookup ref` returns true, `[info exists ref]` in the caller is `1`; on a miss, `0`. So the guard is live code — abstention is the least the fold owes it.

## Root cause

The compiler's upvar-callee context (`detect_upvar_procs` / the CFG builder's per-call-site widening) is built from `ir_module.procedures`. A callee reached by `my`/`next`/object dispatch is a **method**, never in that table — so its `upvar 1 $refvar …` caller-frame definition is invisible, and the caller's defs never widen at the call site.

This is the analyser-side sibling of the family already tracked:
- #1164 covers the *optimiser propagation barrier* consequence (methods can't be in the upvar-proc table → whole-module abstention) and proposes MRO-based reachability;
- #1165 covers the *proc-side* dynamic-local hole in the same table.

This issue is the *diagnostics* consequence: I230/existence and any defs-based check in a method body treat a `my` call to an upvar-performing sibling as a no-op. Pre-existing — unaffected by #1129/#1175 in either direction (measured: the FP is identical before and after).

## Fix shape

The per-class method set is available at lowering (`cu.methods` keyed by class); a conservative first step mirrors the propagation barrier's evidence rule: when any method of the class (or, correctly, of the MRO — same machinery #1164 needs) can reach the caller frame via `reaches_caller_frame` (the shared evidence source PR #1131 established), widen the caller's defs at every `my`/`next` dispatch site instead of treating it as a no-op. Precision beyond that (per-callee resolution through the member tables) composes with #1164's MRO work.

## Summary

#1322 fixed `[self] methodName` / `[self object] methodName` (TclOO's own same-object dispatch idiom) for the LSP-layer consumers: semantic-token highlighting, go-to-definition, find-references, and the reference-count code lens. It deliberately did **not** touch W308 (the "unknown method" diagnostic) — that check lives in `tcl-compiler`'s own class-hierarchy/dispatch analyser (`rust/tcl-compiler/src/analyser/{commands,handlers,oo}.rs` and friends — 17 files reference `W308` in some form), a materially larger, separate piece of machinery from the four LSP-layer consumers #1322 touched, and out of scope for that fix's size.

## Reproduction

```tcl
oo::class create test {
    method animTick {} {
        return {}
    }
    method anim {} {
        [self] nosuchmethod
        return {}
    }
}
```

`my nosuchmethod` in the same position correctly draws W308. `[self] nosuchmethod` draws nothing — a silent false negative (accepting invalid code), which is arguably worse than the false-abstention symptoms #1322 fixed (those just failed to help; this fails to warn).

```bash
python3 .claude/skills/lsp-client/lsp_client.py --server-dir <fixture-dir> --server-bin target/debug/tcl-lsp-server diagnostics selfref_bad.tcl
# === Diagnostics (0 items) ===
```

## Suggested direction

Same registry-driven mechanism #1322 established: `tcl_registry::CommandRegistry::is_self_receiver_call(cmd, arg)` (new in #1322, `rust/tcl-registry/src/registry.rs`) answers "does this bracketed command substitution denote the current TclOO receiver" without name-matching `"self"`. The W308-producing analyser code needs the same treatment `my`-dispatch already gets there — find where the analyser currently checks `method_dispatch_keyword(...) == Some(MethodDispatchKind::SelfDispatch)` for `my` calls (mirroring `tcl-lsp-core`'s `definition.rs`/`references.rs`/`semantic_tokens.rs`, all now fixed) and extend it the same way. Given the wider surface (17 files touch `W308`), this needs its own investigation to find every producer that needs the extra check, not just the first one found — the same class of "partial resolution masks the gap" trap #1296 recorded for the metaclass cluster.

## Problem

`runtime/rust` stores command-trace sidecars by display fully-qualified name. During activation-delayed namespace deletion, recreating a namespace and command with the same FQN lets old and new command incarnations collide:

- binding the fresh command fires the old command token's delete trace immediately;
- finalizing the retained old namespace later fires/removes the fresh command's trace;
- trace delivery order diverges from Tcl 9.0.4 and the native VM.

## Tcl 9.0.4 oracle

```tcl
set log {}
proc rec {tag old new op} {lappend ::log [list $tag $old $new $op]}
namespace eval N {
    proc q {} {return OLDQ}
    trace add command q delete [list ::rec OLD]
    proc p {} {
        namespace delete ::N
        namespace eval ::N {
            proc q {} {return NEWQ}
            trace add command q delete [list ::rec NEW]
        }
        list [q] [::N::q] $::log
    }
}
set answer [::N::p]
set mid $log
rename ::N::q {}
list $answer $mid $log
```

Tcl 9.0.4 and native VM: `{OLDQ NEWQ {}} {{OLD ::N::q {} delete}} {{OLD ::N::q {} delete} {NEW ::N::q {} delete}}`. The standalone runtime fires OLD on fresh bind and NEW during old-token finalization.

## Required contract

- Own command/execution trace sidecars by command token/generation, not display FQN.
- Namespace teardown must deliver traces for the retained old command table only.
- Same-name recreation must not validate, memoize, fire, or remove state belonging to another incarnation.
- Thread the token identity through the shared namespace/command lifecycle layer; do not add a teardown-only string filter.
- Add exact Tcl 9.0.4, native VM, and standalone runtime regression coverage.

Found while reviewing the #1751/#1752/#1753 namespace-lifecycle group. That PR should absorb this fix after the command-mutation PR because retained binding validation and publishing must agree on the same incarnation.

## Root cause

The compiler has separate partial walkers for registry-declared frame effects. They do not share a composed frame context, so late aliases to `eval`/`uplevel`, embedded `VariableCellAlias` transitions, same-frame `upvar 0`, and deeper relative levels can be attributed to the wrong frame or dropped. The current `FrameReach` summary also loses the remaining distance after one wrapper hop.

## Required fix

- Centralize source-safe evaluated-body and variable-cell-alias projection over registry `InvocationFacts`.
- Carry a typed frame context through nested bodies and alias-prefixed invocations.
- Route effects distinctly to current, direct-caller, global, exact-beyond-caller, and unknown frames.
- Close summaries over call edges without extending an exact relative distance indefinitely.
- Cover direct, embedded, late-alias, constructed-list, and dynamic fail-closed forms against Tcl 9.0.4.

## Representative regressions

```tcl
proc p {} {set _ [upvar #0 g local]; set local 1}
proc s {} {uplevel #0 {::upvar 0 g local; ::set local 1}}
proc deep {} {uplevel 3 {::set x 2}}
proc w2 {} {deep}
proc w1 {} {w2}
proc outer {} {set x 1; w1; return $x}
```

Three tests whose bodies do not cover what their names claim. Grouped because the fix is the same shape in each case.

## 1. `irules_alias_dialect_accepted` never uses the alias

`rust/tcl-compiler/src/optimiser/unused_procs.rs:376-387`

```rust
fn irules_alias_dialect_accepted() {
    ...
    let opts = run_pass(source, Some(tcl_dialect::DialectProfile::irules()), ip);
    assert_eq!(opts.len(), 1);
}
```

It passes `DialectProfile::irules()` directly — the canonical profile, not an alias — which makes it identical in substance to `unused_proc_reported_with_o124`. The alias acceptance path it is named for is never exercised.

The gate itself is covered from the other side by `dialect_gate_rejects_non_irules`, so this is a coverage hole rather than an untested feature.

**Fix:** construct the profile through the alias (`is_irules_dialect` accepts the `irules` alias spelling), or rename the test to what it actually pins.

## 2. Entry-protocol test only exercises one protocol

`rust/tcl-compiler/src/native_lowering/tests.rs:549-552`

```rust
fn the_top_level_script_and_a_procedure_body_take_different_entry_protocols() {
    let (top, _) = lower("set a 1\n", native_config()).expect("lowers");
    assert_eq!(top.protocol, EntryProtocol::Script);
}
```

The name promises a contrast between two protocols. The body lowers a script and asserts `EntryProtocol::Script`. Nothing constructs or asserts `EntryProtocol::Procedure`, so the "different" half is untested.

**Fix:** lower a procedure body too and assert it takes `EntryProtocol::Procedure`.

## 3. `empty_source_returns_empty_chain` asserts a tautology

`rust/tcl-lsp-core/src/selection_range.rs:557-562`

```rust
fn empty_source_returns_empty_chain() {
    let ranges = selection_range("", 0, 0, None);
    // No word and no line content: the chain must still be built without
    // panicking.
    assert!(ranges.is_empty() || !ranges.is_empty());
}
```

`x || !x` is true for every value, so the assertion tests nothing beyond "the call did not panic". The name is also wrong in the other direction: the code always pushes a document range for empty input, so the chain is *not* empty.

**Fix:** assert the actual contract — one document range covering the empty document — and rename to match. If "does not panic" really is the whole intent, drop the assertion and say so in the name.

## How these were found

Surfaced during a repository-wide comment review, where each test's doc comment was checked against its body. Reported rather than changed, since fixing them means writing test code.

## What happens

```tcl
proc g {} {}
namespace eval :: {namespace export g}
namespace eval n {namespace import ::g}
namespace eval n {namespace forget ::g}
info commands n::g
```

tclsh 8.6 and 9.0 print nothing (the import is gone); `runtime/rust` prints `::n::g`.

## Where

`runtime/rust/src/cmd_namespace.rs` ~470 (`ns_forget`; the join at ~491): the pattern for a command imported from `::` is joined as `::::name`, which matches no imported command, so nothing is removed.

## Expected

`namespace forget` with a fully qualified pattern whose namespace is `::` removes the matching imported commands, as Tcl does; the join must not double the separator for the global namespace.

## Program

```tcl
proc p {c} {
    if {$c} { set x 1 }
    if {[info vars x] ne ""} { puts $x }
}
p 1
p 0
```

tclsh 8.4 to 9.1: `1`, then nothing (exit 0). `tcl diag`: `3:37 W210 Variable 'x' is read before it is set`.

## Why it is wrong

`docs/kcs/codes/kcs-diagnostic-w210-variable-read-before-set.md` says "The same narrowing applies to the `info vars` / `info locals` membership idioms … and to `catch {set _ $X}`", while `docs/design/compiler/sccp-core-analyses.md` says membership idioms are not recognised as existence proofs, and the analyser has no idiom recognition (`rust/tcl-compiler/src/analyser/diagnostics/` has no reader of `info vars`). The two pages contradict each other and the note describes a guard the code does not honour. Either the note loses the sentence, or the idioms become existence guards through the registry's condition transfer, where `info exists` already is one.

## Program

```tcl
proc p {c} {
    if {$c} {set x 1}
    if {[info exists x] && $x > 0} {puts yes}
}
p 0
```

tclsh 8.4 to 9.1: nothing, exit 0 (`&&` never evaluates `$x` when `x` is unbound). `tcl diag`: `3:8 W210 Variable 'x' is read before it is set`. The form `[info exists y] || $y == 0` draws the same.

## Why it is wrong

The read is the condition's own `$x > 0`, which runs only when the left operand of `&&` holds. Every guard's region starts at the block the true edge enters, so a read inside the condition itself lies outside it, and the existence fact at the condition's statement is `MayBound`. Since W210 reports each variable once, at its first read, this false positive also hides a real unguarded read later in the same procedure: with a following `if {[info exists x] || …} {puts $x}` the second read raises under tclsh and draws nothing. The condition transfer already knows the right operand of `&&` (and of `||`, negated) runs under the left operand's edge facts; stating those facts for the condition's own reads would close it, and the dedupe should not let a false first report swallow a later true one.

## Repro

```tcl
when CLIENT_ACCEPTED {
    sharedvar mode
    set mode 1
}
when SERVER_CONNECTED {
    sharedvar mode
    if {$mode == 1} { log local0. "one" }
    set mode 2
}
```

`tcl opt --profile full --dialect f5-irules` removes both `set mode …` lines (`# O126 Remove unused variable assignment` for the first, `# O109 Eliminate dead store` for the last), so the connection-shared value is never written. `tcl diag --dialect f5-irules` reports `W211 Variable 'mode' is set but never used` at the first assignment and `W220 Assignment to 'mode' is never read` at the last. The same with the two events in the other order and with a third event reading `mode`.

## Where

`sharedvar` declares a scope alias onto the connection's shared storage: the registry's declaration (`CREATES_SCOPE_ALIAS`, the `scope-alias:connection` plan) and the lattice treat the name as escaping. O126 and O109 (`rust/tcl-compiler/src/optimiser/`) and the W211 and W220 analysers do not read that plan, so the connection-shared cell is a plain local to them. `my variable` and `variable` keep their writes.

Identical under the binary built from `e355162ca`.

## Repro

```tcl
package require struct::set
package require struct::list
proc inc {} {set s {}; struct::set include s a; return $s}
proc exc {} {set s {a b}; struct::set exclude s a; return $s}
proc add {} {set s {a}; struct::set add s {b}; return $s}
proc sub {} {set s {a b}; struct::set subtract s {a}; return $s}
proc shf {} {set l {a b c}; struct::list shift l; return $l}
proc asg {} {set x 0; struct::list assign {1 2} x y; return $x}
foreach p {inc exc add sub shf asg} {puts "$p: [$p]"}
```

tclsh 8.5.19, 8.6.18, 9.0.4 and 9.1.0 with tcllib 2.0 print `inc: a exc: b add: a b sub: b shf: b c asg: 1`.

`tcl opt --profile full` makes six O100 rewrites (the `return $s` / `return $x` reads inlined from the store before the call), and the optimised program prints `inc:  exc: a b add: a sub: a b shf: a b c asg: 0`.

## Where

`struct::set include`, `exclude`, `add` and `subtract`, and `struct::list shift` and `assign`, write the caller's variable named by their argument, but their spec modules under `rust/tcl-registry/src/commands/tcllib/` declare no `VarWrite` role for it and inherit `Traits::PURE`, so the call is a pure statement to the lattice and the store before it survives the call. The declarations need the write (and, once declared, the `MayWriteSemantics` the other tcllib writers carry).

Identical under the binary built from `4872a52c7`, before slice 7 of the value-transfer work.
